WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.480
OK, so now we have an idea for a
different algorithm,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.100
and it's going to be based off of an
approach called “branch and bound”.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.070
So let's learn a little bit about this and
I'm going to give a

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:13.260
really hazy, vague overview of what a
branch and bound algorithm's idea is.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.570
So, you're going to start with a

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.150
simple candidate solution and you're going
to “expand” it

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.600
or “branch” that out into different

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.550
solutions, and then you might do this
again.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.560
Then, after you've branched out into a
number

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.160
of possible solutions, or small seeds of
larger possible solutions,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.950
then you're going to say, OK, can we
exclude any of those?

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.320
Can we eliminate any of these candidate
solutions that we have?

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.550
And that's called the “bound step”.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.770
So from here to here we've eliminated

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.430
five solutions, and we're left with, these
are

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.330
not necessarily solutions, but they're at
least candidates

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.310
for solutions, at the leaves of this tree.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.522
Then we say, okay let's branch again, and
then we'll need to bound.

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.850
Based off of some condition, we'll exclude some of
these

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.870
edges when we move from branch to bound.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.230
And now we have, maybe some candidate
solutions,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.890
and we can go ahead and test these.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:06.910
So we've found a way of

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:11.620
eliminating certain solutions, before we
construct them.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.770
So we want to apply the same idea to
eliminating

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.930
some candidate peptides, before they ever
get generated.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.110
Let's apply it to cyclopeptide
sequencing, then.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.680
Here's a possible spectrum.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.080
And our first question is,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.310
which amino acids have masses in the
spectrum?

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:37.020
So if we bring back this integer
mass table, then we highlight, P has mass 97,

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.550
and it occurs in the Spectrum, V has
mass 99, it occurs in the Spectrum.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.019
We also get T and C.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.660
So we're going to start with four 1-mer
peptides.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.370
We're going to start with just these
single amino acids, and what we're going to

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:58.510
do is, we're going to extend these four
into all possible 2-mer peptides,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:03.710
all possible amino acid strings of
length 2. So we get PA, VA, TA, and CA.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:08.980
We get PC, VC, TC, and CC, and so on.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.760
So, we started with four, and we're going
to

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.560
now multiply that by 18, because we're
adding each possible amino acid

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.870
onto each of these four and we're
left with 72.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.670
So, we want to know how we can trim this
list.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.710
So we've done our branch step, and now we need

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.440
to do our bound step and bring it
back.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.220
To do this trimming, we'll notice

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.900
that PV is what's called “consistent” with
Spectrum.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.690
Here's one possible 2-mer, and

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.240
you'll notice that the mass of P is equal
to 97, it’s present.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.810
The mass of V, the other
letter in PV,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.850
is present as 99, and the mass of
this

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:50.860
entire small linear peptide, PV, is 196,
and its also detected.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.780
So that's called “consistent”, if when
you chop up this candidate solution, and

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.690
weigh its fragments, that all those
fragments are found in the spectrum.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:04.390
CD is one that we actually created, in

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.490
the expand step, but it's inconsistent
with this spectrum.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:13.760
So although 103, the mass of C, is present,
is detected, 115 and 218,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.150
the mass of D and the mass of the entire
2-mer peptide CD, they're not found.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.340
You can't find 115 or 218 in this
spectrum. So we want to exclude CD.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.940
After we do this step, and

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.880
we figure out what it is that we need to trim

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.760
we're left with ten amino acid
strings of length two.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.530
From our 72, we've cut it down to ten.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.930
So let's do this exact same thing.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.655
Let's expand each of these in all 18
possible directions.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:47.660
We'll get now, 180 different 3-mer
peptides, and then let's check

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.440
whether or not each one is consistent, and
we'll trim it down.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.270
After we do this,

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:56.570
we go from 180 to 15, and we say, OK, this

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:01.930
is going well, we're preventing these
peptides from growing crazily.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.710
So we expand them, and then we'll
trim.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.460
We go from 15 and do 15 times 18 to get 270.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.073
And then we find which of those 270 4-mers

00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:14.520
are consistent with the spectrum, and
we're down to ten.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.460
And we do it one more time,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.080
expand and then trim, and we're left with
ten consistent 5-mers.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.365
And you can verify that

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.240
each of these

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.920
corresponds to the exact same cyclic
peptide,

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:32.530
and the spectrum of this

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.750
cyclic peptide coincides exactly with our
experimental spectrum.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.240
So now we have a method that's going to
work

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.829
and it looks like it's going to be pretty
efficient.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.780
I'll just summarize what it is.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.960
We find all the amino acids whose masses
occur

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.700
in the spectrum, and we add them to a list.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.730
Then we extend every peptide that's

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:52.760
currently in the list in every possible
direction.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.210
So we add each of 18 different amino acid

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.390
masses, onto the end of it, and then we
have

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:00.860
this trim step, where we say, let's take
which of

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.589
these are inconsistent and let's just get
rid of them.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.320
Then we'll return any peptides whose
theoretical

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.950
spectra match the experimental spectrum
that we're given.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:15.335
And we'll do steps 2 through 4 over
and over again, this branching and bounding

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.930
and then testing, until we run out of

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:21.719
peptides to consider, until we have all
inconsistent peptides.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.990
We've seen it works really well on an example,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.880
but we ask ourselves whether or not this
algorithm is efficient in practice?

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.660
The brute force algorithm that we saw
before, it was exponential, and by

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.970
“exponential” I mean, if you look at its
input size, the number of steps

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.060
that it has to take or, in
this case, the number

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.790
of peptides that it has to consider,

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.120
grows exponentially with respect to the
input size.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.600
It may be true that the branch and bound
algorithm

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.450
that we've come up with is exponential on
some dataset.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.540
Like, somewhere way out in the galaxy you
can form some dataset that takes a

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.590
really, really long time to run and is prohibitive.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.830
But in practice, it's
actually really fast.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.550
So, it looks like we're done and, we can
have a party and go home.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:11.720
That's the question though:

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:12.390
can we really?

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.120
And I'm going to have to pop that balloon
and say “no”.

